<docs-article
  title="Working with AI"
  description="One command gives your coding agent the marko-ui CLI workflow, your installed component list, and the Marko 6 rules it gets wrong by default."
  href="/docs/working-with-ai"
  toc=TOC
>
  <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>

  <p>
    Without marko-ui-specific context, a coding model's defaults are React: it invents
    <code>useState</code>, imports from <code>react</code>, and proposes props that don't
    exist on Marko components. Marko 6 is a different paradigm — resumable, tag-based,
    with a state model built around serialization — and off-the-shelf guidance for it is
    scarce.
  </p>

  <p>
    marko-ui fixes this with two things in your project: a short section in
    <code>AGENTS.md</code> that names your distribution, your installed components, and
    the CLI commands to use, and a set of agent skills that the agent loads on demand
    (the CLI workflow, Marko 6 authoring rules). One command installs both. See
    <a href="/docs/cli">CLI</a> for the full command reference.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quick-start">Quick Start</h2>

  <code-block language="bash" code=SETUP_CMD/>

  <p>
    This one command works in every case. On a new project it runs the full
    <code>init</code> first. On a project that already has a <code>components.json</code>
    it prints <code>components.json already exists — skipping project setup and syncing the agent docs.</code>
    and does only the agent setup. It is safe to re-run, and it exits 0 on success.
  </p>

  <p>It does two things:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      Writes (or updates) a marker-delimited <code>## marko-ui components</code>
      section in <code>AGENTS.md</code> at the project root.
    </li>
    <li>
      Installs the agent skills with the <a href="https://skills.sh" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer external">skills</a>
      package: <code>marko-ui</code> and <code>marko6</code> always, plus
      <code>marko-run</code> in an <code>@marko/run</code> project.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Commit the files it creates, so every teammate and every agent session starts
    with the same context:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>AGENTS.md</code></li>
    <li><code>skills-lock.json</code></li>
    <li><code>.agents/</code> — the canonical skill copies</li>
    <li>
      the agent-specific links, such as <code>.claude/skills/</code> for Claude Code
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If you use Claude Code, also read <a href="#claude-code">Claude Code</a> below: it
    does not read <code>AGENTS.md</code> on its own.
  </p>

  <h3>Or let your agent do it</h3>

  <p>Paste this into your agent. It runs the same command and checks the result:</p>

  <code-block language="text" code=AGENT_PROMPT/>

  <h2 id="what-gets-installed">What Gets Installed</h2>

  <h3>The AGENTS.md section</h3>

  <p>
    The section sits between <code>&lt;!-- marko-ui:start --&gt;</code> and
    <code>&lt;!-- marko-ui:end --&gt;</code> markers. If <code>AGENTS.md</code> does not
    exist it is created. Everything outside the markers is preserved, and re-running
    replaces only what is inside them, so your own agent instructions survive every sync.
  </p>

  <p>The section is deliberately short, because agents load <code>AGENTS.md</code> on every task. It holds:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>the distribution (<code>copy</code> or <code>import</code>) and what that means for editing components;</li>
    <li>the installed components with their descriptions (copy distribution);</li>
    <li>the CLI commands the agent should use — <code>docs</code>, <code>search</code>, <code>add</code>, <code>show</code>, <code>doctor</code>, <code>agents sync</code>;</li>
    <li>an instruction to load the <code>marko-ui</code> and <code>marko6</code> skills before writing component code.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The CLI workflow the agent is taught, from the live manifest:</p>

  <ol>
    <for|step| of=CLI_MANIFEST.agentWorkflow>
      <li><code>${step}</code></li>
    </for>
  </ol>

  <h3>The skills</h3>

  <p>
    The skills come from the <code>skills/</code> directory of the
    <a href="https://github.com/svallory/marko-ui" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer external">marko-ui repository</a>
    and are installed by the <code>skills</code> package, not written by the CLI.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>marko-ui</code> — the CLI workflow, component conventions, and pitfalls.</li>
    <li>
      <code>marko6</code> — the Marko 6 language and an agent-error catalog. It comes
      along because Marko 6 syntax is what agents get wrong first, and every component
      you add is Marko 6 code.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>marko-run</code> — routing and handlers. Added only when the project is an
      <code>@marko/run</code> app.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>Where they land:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>.agents/skills/&lt;name&gt;/</code> — the canonical copy. Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI,
      and other agents that follow the shared convention read it directly.
    </li>
    <li>
      Symlinks in agent-specific folders, such as <code>.claude/skills/&lt;name&gt;</code> for Claude Code.
    </li>
    <li><code>skills-lock.json</code> — records what was installed so it can be checked and updated.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If the skills are already installed, the install step is skipped and the command says
    so. If the install fails (offline, GitHub unreachable), the command exits 1
    <em>after</em> writing <code>AGENTS.md</code> and prints the manual command. Pass
    <code>--no-skill</code> to write <code>AGENTS.md</code> only, with no network access.
  </p>

  <h2 id="claude-code">Claude Code</h2>

  <p>
    Claude Code reads <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, not <code>AGENTS.md</code>. The CLI does not
    edit <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, so add one line to it (create the file if needed):
  </p>

  <code-block language="text" code=CLAUDE_MD/>

  <p>
    <code>@AGENTS.md</code> imports the file, so the marko-ui section reaches Claude Code
    without being copied. Making <code>CLAUDE.md</code> a symlink to <code>AGENTS.md</code>
    works too. The skills need no such step: <code>.claude/skills/</code> is where
    Claude Code looks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="manual-installation">Manual Installation</h2>

  <p>
    You do not need the marko-ui CLI to install the skills. Use the
    <code>skills</code> package directly; it opens an interactive agent picker:
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=SKILLS_BUN/>
  <code-block language="bash" code=SKILLS_NPM/>
  <code-block language="bash" code=SKILLS_PNPM/>

  <p>
    Add <code>-y</code> to accept the defaults without the picker. Add
    <code>-g</code> to install for your user account instead of the project:
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=SKILLS_GLOBAL/>

  <p>List everything the repository offers, including <code>marko-run</code> and <code>port-to-marko</code>:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=SKILLS_LIST/>

  <p>To get the <code>AGENTS.md</code> section without installing skills:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code="bunx marko-ui agents sync --no-skill"/>

  <p>
    This needs an initialized project (<code>components.json</code>) and no network.
    Without one it exits 1 with <code>No components.json found</code>; run
    <code>bunx marko-ui init --agents</code> instead. See
    <a href="/docs/installation">Installation</a> for setting up a project.
  </p>

  <h2 id="keeping-it-current">Keeping It Current</h2>

  <p>Refresh the <code>AGENTS.md</code> section after adding or removing components:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code="bunx marko-ui agents sync"/>

  <p>Update the installed skills:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code="bunx skills update"/>

  <p>Gate it in CI:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=CHECK_CMD/>

  <p>
    <code>--check</code> exits 0 with <code>Agent setup is up to date.</code>, or exits
    <strong>3</strong> with <code>Agent setup is out of date: …. Run marko-ui agents sync.</code>
    when the <code>AGENTS.md</code> section is stale relative to the installed components
    or an expected skill is missing (no lock entry, or no <code>.agents/skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md</code>).
    It checks that setup exists and is current; it does not judge whether the content is
    correct. <code>--check --no-skill</code> checks <code>AGENTS.md</code> only. It never
    writes anything.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cli-contract">CLI Contract for Agents</h2>

  <p>
    <code>marko-ui manifest</code> prints a self-describing JSON document of every
    command, argument, and flag introspected from the live program, alongside
    the exit-code and error-code contracts. An agent can call it once to learn the full
    surface in a single step. The <a href="/docs/cli">CLI page</a> is generated from
    this same manifest.
  </p>

  <h3>Exit codes</h3>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Code</th>
        <th>Meaning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <for|code| of=Object.keys(CLI_MANIFEST.exitCodes)>
        <tr>
          <td><code>${code}</code></td>
          <td>${CLI_MANIFEST.exitCodes[code]}</td>
        </tr>
      </for>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>JSON output envelopes</h3>

  <p>
    <code>docs --list --json</code>, <code>doctor --json</code>, and
    <code>registry list --json</code> return typed success envelopes with
    <code>$type</code>, <code>version</code>, <code>ok</code>, and
    <code>data</code>. For example, an empty documentation list looks like:
  </p>

  <code-block language="json" code=JSON_ENVELOPE/>

  <p>
    <code>info --json</code> (alias of <code>status --json</code>) and
    <code>search --json</code> also provide structured output, but currently return
    plain result objects without this envelope. <code>docs &lt;component&gt;</code>
    returns markdown; its JSON option applies to listing components.
  </p>

  <h3>Non-interactive flags</h3>

  <p>
    Use <code>-y / --yes</code> to skip confirmation prompts and
    <code>--silent</code> to mute progress output where supported.
    <code>add --dry-run</code> previews file changes; <code>init --force</code>
    overwrites existing configuration when that is intended. These are command-specific
    options: consult <code>marko-ui manifest</code> or the
    <a href="/docs/cli#commands">generated command reference</a> before using them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="skills">Skills</h2>

  <p>
    The <a href="https://github.com/svallory/marko-ui" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer external">marko-ui repository</a>
    ships four agent skills. <code>init --agents</code> installs the first two (and
    <code>marko-run</code> where it applies); <code>port-to-marko</code> is opt-in.
  </p>

  <h3>marko-ui</h3>

  <p>
    The CLI workflow (search, docs, add, diff, doctor), how to tell the
    <code>copy</code> and <code>import</code> distributions apart, component
    conventions, and the pitfalls agents hit when a component renders but
    misbehaves.
  </p>

  <h3>marko6</h3>

  <p>
    A Marko 6 language skill containing an agent-error catalog verified
    against the marko-ui codebase, plus language-level rules for reactive
    state, serialization constraints, and attr-tag patterns.
  </p>

  <h3>marko-run</h3>

  <p>
    Routing, request handlers, middleware, and deployment targets for
    <code>@marko/run</code>. Covers the adapter model, the route file conventions,
    and Node, static, and Netlify deployment adapters. Installed only in
    <code>@marko/run</code> projects.
  </p>

  <h3>port-to-marko</h3>

  <p>
    A complete workflow for porting a React component library to Marko: inventory
    upstream components, port them with the SSR-safe Zag.js pattern, run mechanical
    parity verification (type check, hydration invariant, visual diff against the
    upstream), and publish through the official contribution or community registry path.
    Not installed by <code>init --agents</code>; add it with
    <code>bunx skills add svallory/marko-ui --skill port-to-marko</code>. See
    <a href="/docs/contributing-a-library#port-with-an-agent">Contributing a Library</a> for the full
    porting flow and the wanted-library list.
  </p>

  <h2 id="markdown-endpoints">Markdown Endpoints</h2>

  <p>
    Every component page is available as markdown at
    <code>/docs/components/&lt;name&gt;.md</code>. The CLI surfaces this directly:
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code="marko-ui docs button   # prints button's docs as markdown"/>

  <p>
    In an agent session, <code>marko-ui docs &lt;component&gt;</code> pulls the
    documentation straight into the agent's context — usage, props, keyboard
    contracts, and examples. The CLI fetches the markdown endpoint for the agent.
  </p>

  <h2 id="check-your-setup">Check Your Setup</h2>

  <p>First the mechanical check. It exits 0 when everything is in place:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=CHECK_CMD/>

  <p>Then confirm the skills are installed:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code="bunx skills list"/>

  <p>
    Then paste this prompt to your agent to verify that the context actually reached it:
  </p>

  <code-block language="text" code=VERIFY_PROMPT/>

  <p>
    A well-configured agent answers all three from <code>AGENTS.md</code>, the loaded
    skills, and <code>marko-ui docs</code>, without a web search. If it can't, see
    <a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>

  <h3>A components.json file already exists</h3>

  <p>
    You ran <code>marko-ui init</code> on an initialized project. With the current CLI,
    <code>bunx marko-ui init --agents</code> handles this case and only does the agent
    setup. With an older CLI, run <code>bunx marko-ui agents sync</code>. To re-run the
    whole init, use <code>init --force</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>No components.json found</h3>

  <p>
    <code>agents sync</code> exits 1 because the project is not initialized (or you ran
    it outside the project root; pass <code>-c &lt;dir&gt;</code> to point at it). Run
    <code>bunx marko-ui init --agents</code>, which does the project setup and the agent
    setup together.
  </p>

  <h3>Could not install the agent skills</h3>

  <p>
    The <code>skills</code> install failed, usually because you are offline or GitHub is
    unreachable. The command has already written <code>AGENTS.md</code> and exits 1 with
    the manual command. Run that command once you are online (see
    <a href="#manual-installation">Manual Installation</a>), or re-run with
    <code>--no-skill</code> to skip the skills.
  </p>

  <h3>The agent ignores the CLI or invents props</h3>

  <p>Work through these in order:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Run <code>bunx marko-ui agents sync --check</code>. Exit 3 means something is missing; run <code>agents sync</code>.</li>
    <li>Using Claude Code? Confirm <code>CLAUDE.md</code> contains <code>@AGENTS.md</code>. See <a href="#claude-code">Claude Code</a>.</li>
    <li>Tell the agent explicitly: "Load the marko-ui skill, then run <code>marko-ui docs &lt;name&gt;</code> before using a component."</li>
    <li>Start a new session. Agents read instructions and skills at session start.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Upgrading from marko-ui 0.4.1 or earlier</h3>

  <p>
    Those versions wrote a Claude-only skill at <code>.claude/skills/marko-ui/SKILL.md</code>,
    with no lock entry. Run <code>bunx marko-ui agents sync</code>: it replaces that file
    with the symlinked skill from the <code>skills</code> package and records it in
    <code>skills-lock.json</code>. Commit the result.
  </p>
